# STAGE C — Cutover + cleanup (remove legacy env system; harden rules)

**Prompt**

> You are working in the deterministic sim.
> Non-negotiable invariants:
>
> * Determinism suite must pass.
> * No legacy env system remains referenced after this stage.
> * Time travel, projection, and serialization remain correct.
>
> Objective:
>
> * Fully cut over to the new board env system and remove the old envSlots/envSeasons/deck/discard flow.
> * Harden multi-span overlap rules and simplify code paths.
>
> Required work:
>
> 1. Remove legacy env system:
>
>    * Delete/deprecate `envSeasons`, deck/discard, draw/refill code paths.
>    * Remove envSlots iteration from the simulation update loop.
>    * Remove `cmdRefillEnvSlot` and any deck/discard season-end hooks that are no longer meaningful.
> 2. Unify rendering and interaction:
>
>    * Views should exclusively read from `state.board`.
>    * Remove legacy envSlots UI affordances.
> 3. Overlap/placement invariants:
>
>    * Define and enforce policies:
>
>      * tile vs tile overlap: forbidden
>      * permanent vs permanent overlap: forbidden
>      * card vs card overlap: replace policy (deterministic)
>      * card over tile: allowed
>    * Ensure `rebuildBoardDerived` detects impossible states and resolves deterministically (log warnings).
> 4. Remove dead code / rename:
>
>    * Consolidate env defs naming (env tiles vs env events).
>    * Update scenarios to place tiles/permanents using new shapes only.
>    * Update docs/comments.
>
> Constraints:
>
> * Keep the patch coherent; do not leave partially migrated references.
> * Avoid unrelated refactors outside env/board systems.
>
> Deliverables:
>
> * Code changes.
> * Smoke test checklist:
>
>   1. determinism suite
>   2. run app; verify env events still spawn and behave
>   3. scrub timeline; verify stable
>   4. verify no references to envSlots/envSeasons remain
>
> Acceptance criteria:
>
> * Determinism suite passes.
> * Legacy env code removed without breaking sim/replay/projection.
> * Board occupancy rules are enforced and stable.

